  "answer": "Both tests align with my implementation:\n\n1. `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` deep-equals `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }` ✓\n2. `formatValue(12.345)` → `\"12.35\"` ✓\n3. `formatValue(12.34, { precision: 1, prefix: '~' })` → `\"~12.3\"` ✓\n4. `resolveOptions({ unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: 'kg' }` ✓\n5. `formatValue(12.3, { unitLabel: 'kg' })` → `\"12.30 kg\"` ✓\n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the formatting options across all three modules while preserving the existing exports and behavior.\n\n**`defaults.mjs`**\n- `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` now exposes all three defaults: `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }`, wrapped in `Object.freeze` so it can never be mutated.\n\n**`resolver.mjs`**\n- `resolveOptions(overrides)` no longer mutates `DEFAULT_OPTIONS`; it builds and returns a **fresh** object each call.\n- `undefined` means no overrides; `null`, non-objects, and arrays throw `TypeError`.\n- Unknown keys throw `TypeError`.\n- `precision` must be an integer 0–4 (`RangeError` otherwise); `prefix` and `unitLabel` must be strings (`TypeError` otherwise).\n- Only own fields of `overrides` are applied (via `hasOwnProperty`).\n\n**`formatter.mjs`**\n- `formatValue(value, overrides)` rejects non-finite/non-number values with `TypeError`, delegates option resolution to `resolveOptions`, and appends `\" \" + unitLabel` only when `unitLabel` is nonempty.\n- Old call forms (`formatValue(12.345)`, `{ precision, prefix }`) still work without the new field.\n\nAll existing exports (`DEFAULT_OPTIONS`, `resolveOptions`, `formatValue`) are preserved, and no calls or returned objects share mutable state.\n\nNote: I couldn't execute `node --test` since no shell/run tool is available in this environment, but I verified the implementation by tracing each requirement and the test assertions against the code.",
